  "text": "Mór is an Irish goddess who became a figure of folktale. Her name means big or great. She was the land goddess of the province of Munster. Her full name, Mór Mumhan, means the great one of Munster. The word Mór was used for many territorial goddesses. In one tale Mór came to a promontory in County Kerry with her husband Lear, who may be the sea god Lir. They built a house below Mount Eagle. The sea gave them everything they wished. One day Mór climbed the mountain and saw how large her land was. The ravines in the Munster hills are said to come from her streams of urine. Her sons took to the sea, and her joy left her. She withered in sadness, and her temper drove her husband away. The shore where she landed is called Dunmore Head, from the Irish Dún Mór, her fortress. Local folk named it Mary Geeran's house, a place tied to the sun. One tale says Mór went mad, and the king of Cashel had to tame her. This is the same tale told of Mis, the goddess of the Slieve Mish mountains that cut across that ridge. In the Scottish Highlands a giant woman named Mór was the daughter of Smûid.",
